  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}4{x}^{6}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{\u2032},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x & \\,\u27f9\\,{u}^{\u2032}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{\u2032}=4{x}^{6} & \\,\u27f9\\,v=\\int 4{x}^{6}\\,dx=\\frac{4{x}^{7}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}4{x}^{6}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}u{v}^{\u2032}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}v{u}^{\u2032}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x(\\frac{4{x}^{7}}{7}){|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}(\\frac{4{x}^{7}}{7})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\frac{4({e}^{2})^{7}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{2})}{7}-\\frac{4(1)^{7}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1)}{7}]-\\frac{4}{7}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{14}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{2})}{7}-\\frac{4{x}^{7}}{49}{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{e}^{14}}{7}-(\\frac{4{e}^{14}}{49}-\\frac{4}{49}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{49}(56{e}^{14}-4{e}^{14}+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{49}(13{e}^{14}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
